# CI: apply 0062 (record 0061 in the ledger) in the CI database.
#
# WHY THIS PATCH EXISTS
# --------------------
# Migration `0062_record_0061_in_the_ledger.sql` writes exactly one
# row -- the ledger entry for 0061, which 0061 itself forgot to write --
# and nothing else. It is ALREADY APPLIED TO LIVE (PSQL_RC=0, all four
# proofs confirmed, GUS_LEDGER_0061_OK=1).
#
# A migration that is not in the CI chain is a migration that is never
# applied in CI, in the Nightly, or in the local reproduction -- and a
# schema that does not exist. 0062 is already added to TWO of the three
# lists in this PR:
#
#     scripts/ci-migration-chain.sh   (the Nightly database)
#     scripts/repro-ci-db.sh          (the LOCAL reproduction)
#
# This patch adds it to the THIRD list, `.github/workflows/ci.yml`,
# which is the CI database.
#
# WHY IT IS A PATCH AND NOT A PUSH
# --------------------------------
# The GitHub App (`gustale-bot`) has NO `workflows` scope. GitHub
# refuses to let it create or update a file under `.github/workflows/`:
#
#     ! [remote rejected] ... (refusing to allow a GitHub App to create
#       or update workflow `.github/workflows/ci.yml` without
#       `workflows` permission)
#
# The PAT is revoked and the SSH deploy key is rejected, so there is no
# other machine identity that can push a workflow change. This patch is
# therefore a FILE that the owner applies, exactly like the other
# `docs/ci-*.patch` files in this repository.
#
# HOW TO APPLY IT
# ---------------
#     git checkout main && git pull
#     git apply docs/ci-0062-migration-chain.patch
#     git commit -m "ci: apply 0062 (record 0061 in the ledger)"
#     git push
#
# Or, from the PR branch, on top of the migration:
#
#     git apply docs/ci-0062-migration-chain.patch
#
# VERIFYING IT AFTER IT LANDS
# ---------------------------
#     python3 scripts/validate-ci-migration-chain.py
#
# should report that all three lists agree. The count goes from 60
# migrations to 61 -- 0062 is the one that is added.
#
# The chain validator is what proves the three lists stay in step. It
# already passes with the two lists this PR can change; this patch
# makes it three.
#
diff --git a/.github/workflows/ci.yml b/.github/workflows/ci.yml
index 7244091..b8b69ad 100644
--- a/.github/workflows/ci.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/ci.yml
@@ -1338,6 +1338,22 @@ jobs:
             -f db/migrations/0060_legacy_associations_published.sql 2>&1 | tee /tmp/m0060.log
           bash scripts/check-ci-markers.sh "the legacy associations, published as Unverified" "0060: ok" /tmp/m0060.log
 
+      # ── 0062: record 0061 in the ledger ───────────────────────────
+      #
+      # 0061 was applied to live on 2026-11-19 without writing its own
+      # ledger row. An applied migration is frozen (Rule 11), so the gap
+      # is closed in a NEW number rather than by editing 0061.
+      #
+      # It writes ONE row -- the ledger entry for 0061 -- and nothing
+      # else. Its P4 proof checks that the alias table 0061 created still
+      # holds exactly its one row: a ledger migration must not disturb
+      # the data.
+      - name: Record 0061 in the ledger
+        run: |
+          PGPASSWORD=gustale psql -h 127.0.0.1 -U postgres -d gustale_ci -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 \
+            -f db/migrations/0062_record_0061_in_the_ledger.sql 2>&1 | tee /tmp/m0062.log
+          bash scripts/check-ci-markers.sh "record 0061 in the ledger" "GUS_LEDGER_0061_OK" /tmp/m0062.log
+
       # The PER-DISH backfill assertions. LAST, because they assert the
       # state the whole chain produces: the reference dishes'
       # association rows AND the journey places in the editorial queue.
